What is the effect of leaving out the dependency array entirely?

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Clicked ${count} times`;
});
```

In development, inside `<StrictMode>`, this component mounts. What is logged?

```jsx
function Room() {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.log('mount');
    return () => console.log('cleanup');
  }, []);
  return null;
}
```

The counter below is supposed to increase every second. What does the user actually see?

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);
  return <p>{count}</p>;
}
```

The parent re-renders this component three times with unchanged props. How many times in total does the effect run, including the first mount?

```jsx
function Profile({ t }) {
  const options = { id: 1 };
  useEffect(() => {
    subscribe(options);
    return () => unsubscribe(options);
  }, [options]);
  return null;
}
```

What happens when this component mounts?

```jsx
function Loop() {
  const [n, setN] = useState(0);
  useEffect(() => {
    setN(n + 1);
  });
  return <p>{n}</p>;
}
```

A profile page fetches the selected user. When the user switches quickly from A to B, the page sometimes shows A's profile under B's name. Which change fixes it?

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  fetchUser(userId).then(u => setUser(u));
}, [userId]);
```

`Parent` renders `<Child />`. Each component logs during render and has an effect that logs (no StrictMode). Put the logs from the first mount in order.

```jsx
function Child() {
  console.log('render Child');
  useEffect(() => console.log('effect Child'));
  return null;
}
function Parent() {
  console.log('render Parent');
  useEffect(() => console.log('effect Parent'));
  return <Child />;
}
```

Two sibling components each log in `useLayoutEffect` and in `useEffect`. What is logged when they first mount?

```jsx
function X({ n }) {
  useLayoutEffect(() => console.log('layout ' + n));
  useEffect(() => console.log('passive ' + n));
  return null;
}
// <div><X n={1} /><X n={2} /></div>
```

Why does this effect reconnect after every render, and what is the best fix?

```jsx
function ChatRoom({ roomId, serverUrl }) {
  function createOptions() {
    return { roomId, serverUrl };
  }
  useEffect(() => {
    const conn = createConnection(createOptions());
    conn.connect();
    return () => conn.disconnect();
  }, [createOptions]);
}
```

A developer silences the lint warning to keep an empty dependency array, even though the effect reads the `roomId` prop.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const conn = connect(roomId);
  return () => conn.close();
  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, []);
```
Which two consequences follow? (Choose two.)

With React 19.2+, this component mounts with `theme="light"`. About 30 ms later the parent re-renders it with `theme="dark"`. How many intervals are created, and which themes do the ticks report?

```jsx
function Ticker({ theme }) {
  const onTick = useEffectEvent(() => report(theme));
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => onTick(), 10);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);
  return null;
}
```

A list component must reset `selection` when the `items` prop changes. Compare adjusting state during render with doing it in an effect.

```jsx
// Version 1: during render
const [prev, setPrev] = useState(items);
if (items !== prev) { setPrev(items); setSelection(null); }

// Version 2: in an effect
useEffect(() => { setSelection(null); }, [items]);
```
Which statement is accurate?

In React 19 a callback ref may return a cleanup function:

```jsx
<div ref={(node) => {
  console.log('ref', node ? 'node' : 'null');
  return () => console.log('ref cleanup');
}} />
```
The callback is an inline arrow, so it is a new function each render. Which two statements are true? (Choose two.)

A `<SearchBox>` keeps its own `query` state and tells the parent about changes like this. What is the main problem compared with calling `onChange` in the input handler?

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  onChange(query);
}, [query, onChange]);
```

A component listens for a window event and needs the current `count` in the handler:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  function onKey(e) { if (e.key === 'Enter') save(count); }
  window.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
}, [count]);
```
What does this do as `count` changes from 0 to 5?

In development under `<StrictMode>`, this component mounts with `q="x"`. `fetchResults` resolves after about 5 ms. How many requests are sent, and how many times does `setData` run?

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  let ignore = false;
  fetchResults(q).then(r => { if (!ignore) setData(r); });
  return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [q]);
```

What does this component log on mount (no StrictMode)?

```jsx
function Box() {
  const [w, setW] = useState(0);
  console.log('render w=' + w);
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    console.log('layout w=' + w);
    if (w === 0) setW(100);
  });
  useEffect(() => console.log('passive w=' + w));
  return null;
}
```

A tooltip must be placed above its anchor, or below it if there is no room. The current code measures in `useEffect` and users see it flash in the wrong position for one frame. Why does switching to `useLayoutEffect` fix this, and what does it cost?

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const { height } = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect();
  setTooltipHeight(height);
}, []);
```

A chat component re-subscribes to a socket on every message because its handler needs the latest `muted` setting:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const off = socket.on('message', (m) => { if (!muted) play(m); });
  return off;
}, [socket, muted]);
```
You want to subscribe once per `socket`, and still respect the current `muted` value. Which version is correct in React 19.2+?

Before `useEffectEvent`, a common pattern kept the latest callback in a ref:

```jsx
const latest = useRef(onTick);
latest.current = onTick; // assigned during render
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => latest.current(), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```
Why does React advise moving `latest.current = onTick` into `useLayoutEffect` (or using `useEffectEvent`)?
